lth advisory and trust services. The company was founded in 1910 and is based in Jasper, Indiana.","ceo_bio":"Mr. D. Neil Dauby CPA | CEO & Chairman | Age 61 | Compensation: $1.6M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 984 people. Revenue per employee of $495K ranks in the below the sector median among Financial Services peers, improving at 13%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $157K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["German American Bancorp, Inc. is a small-cap financial services business with a $1.9B market cap on $487M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +23% over the past three years and +17% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 11% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The business throws off $154M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 32 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.41\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.6\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 1.1\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.3% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 3.2% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.4) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 32% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Insiders are net buyers (100% buy ratio) \u2014 insiders sell for many reasons but buy for only one","32 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Growing faster than most Financial Services peers \u2014 taking share inside the profit pool","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Be Sure To Check Out German American Bancorp, Inc. (NASDAQ:GABC) Before It Goes Ex-Dividend","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/banks/nasdaq-gabc/german-american-bancorp/news/be-sure-to-check-out-german-american-bancorp-inc-nasdaqgabc-1","date":"2026-11-05","timestamp":1793873226.712,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.318,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["dividend"]},{"title":"Zacks Research Upgrades German American Bancorp (NASDAQ:GABC) to \u201cStrong-Buy\u201d","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link